          <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <button class="step-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="body-3">
            <h2>Run it</h2>
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <button class="btn mark" aria-pressed="false">Mark done</button>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-3" hidden>
          <ol>
            <li>In the AI’s reply, find the code block and click its <strong>Copy</strong> button. Don’t select-and-drag - the button gets all of it.</li>
            <li>In TXTEDTR, paste. Then <strong>Save as</strong>: into your <code>vibe-coding</code> folder, named <code>qr-code.html</code>. Lowercase, a hyphen, no spaces, and type the <code>.html</code> yourself.</li>
            <li>Find <code>qr-code.html</code> in your <code>vibe-coding</code> folder and double-click it. It opens in your browser.</li>
            <li>Type something in the box. A QR code should appear. Scan it with your phone’s camera.</li>
          </ol>

          <figure class="tree">
            <pre role="img" aria-label="Folder diagram. The vibe-coding folder contains two files: setup-check.html from the setup lab, and qr-code.html, which is this lab.">vibe-coding/
          ├── setup-check.html
          └── qr-code.html   <span class="here">← this lab</span></pre>
            <figcaption>Your folder after step 2: the check file from setup, and your first real app beside it. The <em>names</em> don’t have to match mine - your folder might be <code>my-apps</code>, the file might be <code>qr-maker.html</code>. You’ll see diagrams like this all semester; they read top-down, and <code>└──</code> means “inside the folder above.”</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p class="aside">This page needs the internet even though it’s a file on your computer - it fetches the QR library from the web when it opens. If you’re offline it shows nothing. You’ll learn why in a later chapter.</p><p>You’re not going to understand this file today, and you’re not going to change it by hand.</p>

          <h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
          <p>Every lab has a few ways to go sideways. None of them mean you did something wrong - they mean you’re doing the lab. Scan the titles for what you’re seeing and open that one.</p>

          <h4 class="group">Start here</h4>

          <details class="note">
            <summary>Nothing changed after you fixed something?</summary>
            <div class="note-body">
              <p>Did you save? Did you refresh? Both, every time. Edit → save → switch to the browser → refresh. Most “it’s broken” moments this semester are one of those two steps skipped.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="note" id="console">
            <summary>Open the Developer Console - it tells you what broke, and where</summary>
            <div class="note-body">
              <p>Your browser has a hidden panel that reports exactly what went wrong and <strong>which line it went wrong on</strong> - even when the page itself shows nothing. That’s far more useful than guessing, and it’s what you paste to the AI. Open it with:</p>
              <table class="shortcuts">
                <tr><th scope="row">Chrome, Edge, Brave</th><td><kbd>F12</kbd>, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>J</kbd> (Mac: <kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>⌥</kbd>+<kbd>J</kbd>)</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Firefox</th><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (Mac: <kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>⌥</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>)</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Safari</th><td>Once: Safari → Settings → Advanced → turn on <em>Show features for web developers</em>. Then <kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>⌥</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd></td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Any browser</th><td>Right-click the page → <strong>Inspect</strong> → click the <strong>Console</strong> tab</td></tr>
              </table>
              <p>What you’re looking for: <strong>red lines</strong>. Each one has a message (what broke) and, at the right, a file name and a number like <code>countdown.html:42</code> (where - line 42). Copy the message exactly and paste it to the AI. The line number tells you where to look in your editor if you’re curious.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <h4 class="group">This lab</h4>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>The page shows, but no QR code appears</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Almost always: no internet. The page borrows the QR library from the web when it opens. Check your connection, refresh. If you’re online and it still shows nothing, open the Developer Console (see above) - a red line about a failed script is the library not loading.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="flag">
            <summary>The code appears but my phone won’t scan it</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Two usual causes. <strong>Too pale:</strong> a light color on a white background doesn’t have enough contrast for a camera - pick something darker. <strong>Initials too big:</strong> ask the AI to make the center box smaller, or to raise the error correction level.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="flag">
            <summary>I asked for one change and my earlier change disappeared</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>The AI rebuilt the file and dropped something along the way - your color, your picker. Tell it plainly: <em>“You removed the color picker. Put it back and keep it this time.”</em> Then, in every future ask, end with <em>“keep everything else the same.”</em> This is the most common thing the AI gets wrong, and the fix is one sentence.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="flag">
            <summary>I pasted the new version and now the page is blank</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Usually the paste didn’t replace <em>everything</em> - some of the old file is still there, above or below the new one. In your editor, select all and paste again, then check the file starts with <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> and ends with <code>&lt;/html&gt;</code>. If it’s still blank, scroll up in the chat: every earlier version the AI gave you is still there. Copy the last one that worked, paste it over everything, and you’re back where you were.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <h4 class="group">Every lab</h4>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>A blank page, or red text on it</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>The file is fine. Something in it broke - and a blank page is the browser giving up quietly rather than telling you. <strong>Open the Developer Console</strong> (see above). There will almost always be a red line there, even though the page shows nothing. <strong>Copy the exact message</strong> - not “it’s broken” - and paste it to the AI with “I got this error.” Then copy the fixed code, paste it over everything, save, refresh.</p>
              <p>This is the loop: console → red text → copy it exactly → paste it back. You’ll use it all semester, and the difference between quoting the error and describing it is enormous.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>Double-clicking opens the code, not the page</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Your computer sent the <code>.html</code> file to a text editor. Right-click the file → <strong>Open with</strong> → pick your browser. Or drag the file onto an open browser window.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>The file ends in <code>.html.txt</code></summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Your editor added <code>.txt</code>. If you can’t see file extensions, turn them on (Mac: Finder → Settings → Advanced. Windows: File Explorer → View → File name extensions). Then rename the file to end in <code>.html</code> and nothing else.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>Blank page, and the console is empty too</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>No red line and nothing on the page usually means the browser never got a whole page. Scroll to the bottom of your file - it should end with <code>&lt;/html&gt;</code>. If it doesn’t, you copied only part of the code: go back to the AI’s reply and use the <strong>Copy</strong> button on the code block, not select-and-drag. Also check the file really ends in <code>.html</code> and not <code>.txt</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>The AI gave you two or three separate files</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Sometimes it splits the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript apart even when asked not to. Tell it: <em>“put everything in one HTML file, please.”</em> That line in the specification is there for exactly this.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>You pasted through a Word document or email first</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>Word-processor-shaped things turn straight quotes <code>"</code> into curly ones <code>“ ”</code>, and code can’t read curly quotes. Go back to the AI’s code block and copy it straight into your editor.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="flag">
            <summary>You can’t find the file you saved</summary>
            <div class="flag-body">
              <p>It probably landed in Downloads. Search your computer for the file name you gave it, move it into your course folder, and from now on use <strong>Save as</strong> and pick the folder deliberately.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
