      <!-- ============ 4 - MAKE IT YOURS ============ -->
      <section class="step" data-step="4" data-beat="yours">
        <div class="step-head">
          <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
          <button class="step-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="body-4">
            <h2>Make it yours</h2>
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <button class="btn mark" aria-pressed="false">Mark done</button>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-4" hidden>
          <p>Time to make it yours - by <em>asking</em>.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Back in the same chat, ask:
              <div class="prompt">
                <button class="btn copy" data-copy="prompt-default">Copy prompt</button>
                <pre id="prompt-default">Change the default color of the QR code from black to [your color]. Keep everything else exactly the same.</pre>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>The AI hands back a whole new version of the file. Click its <strong>Copy</strong> button.</li>
            <li>In TXTEDTR, select everything (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>, Mac <kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>) and paste over it. Save.</li>
            <li>Refresh the browser. Make a code. It’s your color. Scan it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You just changed an app, and you never touched the code. Ask, copy, paste over, save, refresh. That’s vibe coding. The rest of the course is that loop with bigger asks.</p>
          <p class="aside"><strong>“Keep everything else exactly the same”</strong> is doing the dirty work in that prompt. Left to itself, an AI asked to change one thing will sometimes redesign three. Saying what <em>not</em> to change is part of asking well - you’ll write that sentence a lot.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <!-- ============ 5 - GROW IT ============ -->
      <section class="step" data-step="5" data-beat="grow">
        <div class="step-head">
          <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
          <button class="step-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="body-5">
            <h2>Grow it</h2>
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <button class="btn mark" aria-pressed="false">Mark done</button>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-5" hidden>
          <p>Two more asks, one at a time. Same loop each time: ask, copy, paste over everything, save, refresh, scan.</p>
          <p><strong>First, a color picker</strong> - so anyone using your page can pick, not just you:</p>
          <div class="prompt">
            <button class="btn copy" data-copy="prompt-picker">Copy prompt</button>
            <pre id="prompt-picker">Add a color picker so the user can choose the color of the QR code. Start it on the color it has now. Keep the background white so the code still scans.</pre>
          </div>
          <p>Pick a few colors. Scan a couple. Notice that a very pale one won’t scan - remember that feeling; there’s a whole chapter on it later.</p>
          <p><strong>Then, your initials:</strong></p>
          <div class="prompt">
            <button class="btn copy" data-copy="prompt-initials">Copy prompt</button>
            <pre id="prompt-initials">Put my initials, [ABC], in the middle of the QR code in a small white box. Use the highest error correction level so the code still scans with the initials covering the center. Keep everything else the same.</pre>
          </div>
          <p>Scan it. It still works.</p>
          <p class="aside"><strong>Neat fact:</strong> QR codes are built to survive damage - up to about 30% of the code can be covered or torn and a phone still reads it. That’s why your initials can sit right on top. You just used a feature designed for scratched posters.</p>
          <p><em>Want a logo instead of initials?</em> Soon. Putting a picture into an app means putting a file beside it, and that’s later in this course. For now, initials.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <!-- ============ 6 - KEEP IT ============ -->
      <section class="step" data-step="6" data-beat="keep">
        <div class="step-head">
          <span class="step-num" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
          <button class="step-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="body-6">
            <h2>Keep it</h2>
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <button class="btn mark" aria-pressed="false">Mark done</button>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-6" hidden>
          <ul>
            <li>Your app is <code>vibe-coding/qr-code.html</code>. Leave it there.</li>
            <!-- BOARD (parked until the Lessons Learned board opens - restore this line, drop the one below):
            <li><strong>Did anything surprise you?</strong> An error, a wrong filename, a thing that took longer than it should have? <strong>Post it to the board</strong> - one sentence of what happened, one of what fixed it.</li>
            -->
            <li><strong>Did anything surprise you?</strong> The AI changed something you didn’t ask about. A color that wouldn’t scan. A paste that missed the top of the file. <strong>Write it down</strong> - one sentence of what happened, one of what fixed it. You’ll be asked for these later, and they’re worth more than they feel right now.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

